         "The impending proliferation of autonomous vehicles promises to revolutionise urban mobility while introducing profound ethical dilemmas regarding accident decisions. || Proponents highlight that self-driving fleets will drastically reduce traffic fatalities caused by human error while optimizing urban space. However, unavoidable collision scenarios force engineers to codify philosophy into code, raising complex liability questions. || Resolving these quandaries requires transparent regulatory standards and clear legal frameworks that assign accountability to manufacturers. || Ultimately, deploying autonomous machines demands extraordinary moral rigor alongside technical excellence.",
